1
00:00:01,956 --> 00:00:03,916
I'm sitting with my client.

2
00:00:03,916 --> 00:00:05,752
Let's call her Lisa.

3
00:00:05,752 --> 00:00:07,462
She looks me straight in the eye

4
00:00:07,462 --> 00:00:11,132
and tells me she's ready for a relationship.

5
00:00:11,132 --> 00:00:13,926
So I ask what she's looking for in a partner.

6
00:00:13,926 --> 00:00:15,595
Someone kind,

7
00:00:15,595 --> 00:00:19,557
emotionally available, family-oriented.

8
00:00:19,557 --> 00:00:20,725
Great.

9
00:00:20,725 --> 00:00:22,143
We're off to a good start.

10
00:00:22,143 --> 00:00:24,687
She then tells me her deal breakers.

11
00:00:24,687 --> 00:00:29,400
No one who’s selfish, dishonest [or] rude to service staff.

12
00:00:29,400 --> 00:00:31,778
Yes, reasonable.

13
00:00:31,778 --> 00:00:35,281
She then adds, no one under six feet tall.

14
00:00:35,281 --> 00:00:38,034
(Laughter)

15
00:00:38,034 --> 00:00:39,827
Hmm.

16
00:00:39,827 --> 00:00:41,162
I’m not here to judge --

17
00:00:41,162 --> 00:00:43,456
we all have our lists.

18
00:00:43,456 --> 00:00:46,584
But the six-foot height requirement?

19
00:00:46,584 --> 00:00:49,295
The math isn't mathin'.

20
00:00:49,295 --> 00:00:54,717
In America, about 14 percent of men are six feet or taller.

21
00:00:54,717 --> 00:00:59,388
Her dating pool just turned into a puddle.

22
00:00:59,388 --> 00:01:02,350
Now I’m not here to just pick on height.

23
00:01:02,350 --> 00:01:04,894
You know, replace that with status,

24
00:01:04,894 --> 00:01:06,312
income, looks,

25
00:01:06,312 --> 00:01:09,690
if they're a Leo or, God forbid, a Gemini.

26
00:01:09,690 --> 00:01:10,900
(Laughter)

27
00:01:10,900 --> 00:01:16,781
The specifics change, but the pattern remains the same.

28
00:01:16,781 --> 00:01:20,326
People say they want love

29
00:01:20,326 --> 00:01:25,790
and then eliminate the very people they can build it with.

30
00:01:25,790 --> 00:01:29,752
I've seen this for two decades as a dating columnist

31
00:01:29,752 --> 00:01:31,879
and running “breakup bootcamps.”

32
00:01:31,879 --> 00:01:34,465
I've helped people who were perpetually single,

33
00:01:34,465 --> 00:01:37,135
stuck in situationship purgatory,

34
00:01:37,135 --> 00:01:40,429
create healthy, lasting relationships.

35
00:01:40,429 --> 00:01:44,433
But only after they stopped doing the very thing

36
00:01:44,433 --> 00:01:46,727
they thought was protecting them.

37
00:01:46,727 --> 00:01:49,272
That's what we're going to unpack.

38
00:01:49,272 --> 00:01:53,734
People are not forming relationships like they used to.

39
00:01:53,734 --> 00:01:57,446
Last year, a study by Match and the Kinsey Institute

40
00:01:57,446 --> 00:02:00,032
found that half of US singles

41
00:02:00,032 --> 00:02:04,370
went on a total of zero dates.

42
00:02:04,370 --> 00:02:06,372
(Laughter)

43
00:02:06,372 --> 00:02:10,918
Researchers are warning we are in a “relationship recession.”

44
00:02:10,918 --> 00:02:12,378
(Laughter)

45
00:02:12,378 --> 00:02:14,881
So what's driving this?

46
00:02:14,881 --> 00:02:18,718
There are three forces working against us:

47
00:02:18,718 --> 00:02:20,052
our brain,

48
00:02:20,052 --> 00:02:23,389
our culture and technology.

49
00:02:23,389 --> 00:02:26,225
Let's start with the brain.

50
00:02:26,225 --> 00:02:30,438
Human beings evolved with negativity bias.

51
00:02:30,438 --> 00:02:31,856
A hundred thousand years ago,

52
00:02:31,856 --> 00:02:35,276
if you missed a threat -- a tiger in the grass,

53
00:02:35,276 --> 00:02:37,737
a snake by your foot --

54
00:02:37,737 --> 00:02:39,238
you could die.

55
00:02:39,238 --> 00:02:43,951
So the brain got really good at scanning for signs of danger.

56
00:02:43,951 --> 00:02:48,247
Now this wiring kept our ancestors alive.

57
00:02:48,247 --> 00:02:55,046
This same wiring is wreaking havoc on our dating lives.

58
00:02:55,046 --> 00:02:57,465
Imagine you're on a date.

59
00:02:57,465 --> 00:03:01,177
About an hour in, the conversation's flowing,

60
00:03:01,177 --> 00:03:03,387
they're laughing at your jokes,

61
00:03:03,387 --> 00:03:06,766
and then they tell you they don't really read books.

62
00:03:06,766 --> 00:03:10,311
They prefer podcasts instead.

63
00:03:10,311 --> 00:03:12,104
(Laughter)

64
00:03:12,104 --> 00:03:15,191
An alarm bell goes off in your brain and you start panicking.

65
00:03:15,191 --> 00:03:17,276
"What else is wrong with this person?"

66
00:03:17,276 --> 00:03:19,320
(Laughter)

67
00:03:19,320 --> 00:03:25,701
Once your brain starts scanning for flaws, it starts finding them everywhere.

68
00:03:25,701 --> 00:03:30,665
Suddenly, the way they chew is a little too loud.

69
00:03:30,665 --> 00:03:32,541
They're not making enough eye contact.

70
00:03:32,541 --> 00:03:34,585
They're making too much eye contact.

71
00:03:34,585 --> 00:03:37,004
Did they just say JIF instead of GIF?

72
00:03:37,004 --> 00:03:39,590
(Laughter)

73
00:03:39,590 --> 00:03:43,135
Your brain decides this person is no longer a romantic prospect

74
00:03:43,135 --> 00:03:46,889
and banished to the ick list they go.

75
00:03:46,889 --> 00:03:50,309
Now if this were just about our brains’ wiring,

76
00:03:50,309 --> 00:03:53,062
we could override this.

77
00:03:53,062 --> 00:03:57,400
But there’s a second force working against us:

78
00:03:57,400 --> 00:03:59,026
our culture.

79
00:03:59,026 --> 00:04:03,239
Our culture has turned flaw-hunting

80
00:04:03,239 --> 00:04:06,534
into a collective sport.

81
00:04:06,534 --> 00:04:09,287
Just scroll through social media. What do you see?

82
00:04:09,287 --> 00:04:11,038
Dating horror stories.

83
00:04:11,038 --> 00:04:13,624
Red flags to look out for.

84
00:04:13,624 --> 00:04:19,088
It's resulted in a generation of daters who are quick to ick,

85
00:04:19,088 --> 00:04:21,757
constantly looking for what could go wrong

86
00:04:21,757 --> 00:04:25,386
instead of what could go right.

87
00:04:25,386 --> 00:04:28,222
Unrealistic expectations

88
00:04:28,222 --> 00:04:33,853
get rebranded as “high standards.”

89
00:04:33,853 --> 00:04:38,899
Quirks become deal breakers.

90
00:04:38,899 --> 00:04:41,819
I see this constantly in my work,

91
00:04:41,819 --> 00:04:46,657
and I'm guilty of it, too.

92
00:04:46,657 --> 00:04:51,412
One Saturday morning, I'm walking with my friend Vishal to get a coffee.

93
00:04:51,412 --> 00:04:53,372
Vishal is a superconnector.

94
00:04:53,372 --> 00:04:55,166
He knows everyone.

95
00:04:55,166 --> 00:04:57,084
I was single for five years,

96
00:04:57,084 --> 00:05:00,171
so I asked if he had anyone to set me up with.

97
00:05:00,171 --> 00:05:03,132
I remember the look of excitement in his eyes when he's like,

98
00:05:03,132 --> 00:05:05,468
"Oh my gosh, I have the best person for you.

99
00:05:05,468 --> 00:05:09,638
He's super smart, he's funny, really good guy."

100
00:05:09,638 --> 00:05:13,059
He then takes out his phone and shows me his picture.

101
00:05:13,059 --> 00:05:14,352
I take a look.

102
00:05:14,352 --> 00:05:15,686
It's a guy with dark hair

103
00:05:15,686 --> 00:05:19,607
and he's leaning back in a chair with half a grin.

104
00:05:19,607 --> 00:05:22,234
He was giving ...

105
00:05:22,234 --> 00:05:24,445
cocky-finance-bro vibes.

106
00:05:24,445 --> 00:05:29,158
(Laughter)

107
00:05:29,158 --> 00:05:33,287
So I hand the phone back to Vishal and asked if he had anyone else.

108
00:05:33,287 --> 00:05:35,748
(Laughter)

109
00:05:35,748 --> 00:05:41,504
Let's just say Vishal never tried to set me up with anyone ever again.

110
00:05:41,504 --> 00:05:45,674
Now I made a snap judgment off of one photo.

111
00:05:45,674 --> 00:05:49,720
But imagine doing this a hundred times a day.

112
00:05:49,720 --> 00:05:55,267
This brings me to the third force: technology.

113
00:05:55,267 --> 00:06:00,106
Dating apps have changed the way we meet people,

114
00:06:00,106 --> 00:06:04,819
but they've also changed the way we think about people.

115
00:06:04,819 --> 00:06:10,491
The endless options train us to assemble partners like Build-a-Bear.

116
00:06:10,491 --> 00:06:14,703
I'll take this height, this income level, this love language,

117
00:06:14,703 --> 00:06:18,332
customize a perfect person and add to cart.

118
00:06:18,332 --> 00:06:20,334
(Laughter)

119
00:06:20,334 --> 00:06:21,836
On top of that,

120
00:06:21,836 --> 00:06:25,172
performative romance on social media

121
00:06:25,172 --> 00:06:28,759
has become the new fairy tale.

122
00:06:28,759 --> 00:06:35,057
We compare real human beings with curated, filtered,

123
00:06:35,057 --> 00:06:37,893
perfected highlight reels.

124
00:06:37,893 --> 00:06:41,730
Of course, nobody measures up.

125
00:06:41,730 --> 00:06:47,111
It all points to the same problem.

126
00:06:47,111 --> 00:06:51,615
We are chasing shiny qualities.

127
00:06:51,615 --> 00:06:59,039
The things that look good on paper, that appeal to the ego.

128
00:06:59,039 --> 00:07:01,417
But guess what?

129
00:07:01,417 --> 00:07:03,544
In six to 24 months,

130
00:07:03,544 --> 00:07:05,588
that chiseled jawline,

131
00:07:05,588 --> 00:07:07,840
that silky-smooth hair?

132
00:07:07,840 --> 00:07:09,967
Your brain adapts.

133
00:07:09,967 --> 00:07:13,929
Psychologists call this hedonic adaptation.

134
00:07:13,929 --> 00:07:17,099
What once drew you in and gave you butterflies

135
00:07:17,099 --> 00:07:20,311
becomes the new normal.

136
00:07:20,311 --> 00:07:27,026
We are over-optimizing for what fades the fastest.

137
00:07:27,026 --> 00:07:32,281
So what actually does predict lasting love?

138
00:07:32,281 --> 00:07:35,534
A professor named Samantha Joel wanted to find out.

139
00:07:35,534 --> 00:07:38,913
So she assembled a team of 85 scientists,

140
00:07:38,913 --> 00:07:42,917
and together, they analyzed data from 11,000 couples.

141
00:07:42,917 --> 00:07:47,171
Turns out, we've been looking for the wrong things.

142
00:07:47,171 --> 00:07:49,673
All that stuff we obsess over

143
00:07:49,673 --> 00:07:52,927
in the early stages of dating, the shiny qualities,

144
00:07:52,927 --> 00:07:56,597
had almost zero ability to predict

145
00:07:56,597 --> 00:08:01,519
if a relationship would be happy or stable.

146
00:08:01,519 --> 00:08:04,855
But you know what did?

147
00:08:04,855 --> 00:08:07,816
The quiet qualities.

148
00:08:07,816 --> 00:08:12,112
Whether your partner is committed to you.

149
00:08:12,112 --> 00:08:15,866
Whether you feel appreciated by them.

150
00:08:15,866 --> 00:08:19,203
How well you handle conflict together.

151
00:08:19,203 --> 00:08:23,958
The stuff that only shows up on a random Tuesday when you’re sick,

152
00:08:23,958 --> 00:08:27,628
and they bring you soup without you having to ask.

153
00:08:27,628 --> 00:08:34,093
We are undervaluing what matters in the long term.

154
00:08:34,093 --> 00:08:38,514
So what do we do about this?

155
00:08:38,514 --> 00:08:43,435
First, create a connection reset.

156
00:08:43,435 --> 00:08:44,895
We know [that] on a date,

157
00:08:44,895 --> 00:08:47,106
your brain is looking for what could go wrong.

158
00:08:47,106 --> 00:08:49,775
But there's an antidote to this.

159
00:08:49,775 --> 00:08:51,485
Awe.

160
00:08:51,485 --> 00:08:56,365
Awe is that feeling of being in the presence of something vast,

161
00:08:56,365 --> 00:08:59,577
something bigger than yourself.

162
00:08:59,577 --> 00:09:05,249
Research shows that experiencing awe makes people more open, more curious,

163
00:09:05,249 --> 00:09:08,210
more connected to others.

164
00:09:08,210 --> 00:09:12,506
And you don't have to wait for a solar eclipse to feel it.

165
00:09:12,506 --> 00:09:16,760
You can actually cultivate awe in micro-doses.

166
00:09:16,760 --> 00:09:20,180
Listen to a song that moves you.

167
00:09:20,180 --> 00:09:22,308
Watch the sunset.

168
00:09:22,308 --> 00:09:28,230
Even watching a video of the Northern Lights can spark awe.

169
00:09:28,230 --> 00:09:31,692
So next time before you go on a date,

170
00:09:31,692 --> 00:09:36,071
spend 15 minutes cultivating awe.

171
00:09:36,071 --> 00:09:41,910
This will prime your brain for connection instead of critique.

172
00:09:41,910 --> 00:09:48,500
Next, know the difference between a deal breaker and a preference.

173
00:09:48,500 --> 00:09:54,089
A deal breaker is something that would make a healthy relationship impossible.

174
00:09:54,089 --> 00:09:57,051
You have different core values.

175
00:09:57,051 --> 00:09:59,845
You want children, they don't.

176
00:09:59,845 --> 00:10:03,474
A preference, though, is a nice-to-have,

177
00:10:03,474 --> 00:10:07,061
not a need-to-have.

178
00:10:07,061 --> 00:10:11,690
Would it be nice if they liked hiking as much as you do?

179
00:10:11,690 --> 00:10:12,900
Sure.

180
00:10:12,900 --> 00:10:15,444
But will it make or break a relationship?

181
00:10:15,444 --> 00:10:17,154
No.

182
00:10:17,154 --> 00:10:21,825
Third, conduct your own dating experiment.

183
00:10:21,825 --> 00:10:25,579
Commit to going on dates with people outside of your type.

184
00:10:25,579 --> 00:10:28,791
Stop putting so much weight on the shiny qualities

185
00:10:28,791 --> 00:10:33,170
and consider what actually matters in a partnership.

186
00:10:33,170 --> 00:10:39,885
Not just in two months, but in 20 years.

187
00:10:39,885 --> 00:10:41,178
One sunny morning,

188
00:10:41,178 --> 00:10:45,933
I’m on my couch, and I’m scrolling through a dating app.

189
00:10:45,933 --> 00:10:47,935
I match with this guy.

190
00:10:47,935 --> 00:10:52,398
He’s got brown hair, a scruffy beard and a warm smile.

191
00:10:52,398 --> 00:10:54,274
After messaging back and forth,

192
00:10:54,274 --> 00:10:57,194
it's apparent that we have a friend in common,

193
00:10:57,194 --> 00:10:59,154
Vishal.

194
00:10:59,154 --> 00:11:04,660
(Laughter)

195
00:11:04,660 --> 00:11:06,328
So I text Vishal.

196
00:11:06,328 --> 00:11:09,540
"Hey, do you know this guy named Paul?"

197
00:11:09,540 --> 00:11:11,375
Vishal calls me right away. He’s like,

198
00:11:11,375 --> 00:11:14,002
"Amy, I tried to set you up with him and you said no."

199
00:11:14,002 --> 00:11:16,463
(Laughter)

200
00:11:16,463 --> 00:11:21,260
“Remember the photo of the cocky finance bro?”

201
00:11:21,260 --> 00:11:25,222
Turns out that was taken when Paul face-planted on the bunny hill

202
00:11:25,222 --> 00:11:27,558
while learning how to ski.

203
00:11:27,558 --> 00:11:29,184
Oops.

204
00:11:29,184 --> 00:11:32,771
We've now been together for seven years.

205
00:11:32,771 --> 00:11:39,194
(Applause)

206
00:11:39,194 --> 00:11:42,740
It's the healthiest relationship I've ever been in.

207
00:11:42,740 --> 00:11:48,579
But not by Instagram standards.

208
00:11:48,579 --> 00:11:59,089
A few years ago, my father got really sick.

209
00:11:59,089 --> 00:12:07,222
I spent a year helping him in his last stage of life.

210
00:12:07,222 --> 00:12:10,768
We were in and out of the emergency room,

211
00:12:10,768 --> 00:12:13,353
and I was so overwhelmed.

212
00:12:13,353 --> 00:12:17,733
I felt like I was breaking into a million pieces.

213
00:12:17,733 --> 00:12:23,739
I was a shell of a human being and not fun to be around.

214
00:12:23,739 --> 00:12:28,076
Paul never made me feel like I was too much.

215
00:12:28,076 --> 00:12:31,371
He knew exactly when I needed a hug

216
00:12:31,371 --> 00:12:33,874
or when I needed space.

217
00:12:33,874 --> 00:12:36,502
He would cook me meals.

218
00:12:36,502 --> 00:12:39,755
And because he knows that I always forget to drink water,

219
00:12:39,755 --> 00:12:42,591
every morning, he would fill a pitcher of water

220
00:12:42,591 --> 00:12:45,344
and place it on my desk.

221
00:12:45,344 --> 00:12:49,973
He still does that to this day.

222
00:12:49,973 --> 00:12:54,436
Apparently my love language is hydration.

223
00:12:54,436 --> 00:12:56,772
(Laughter)

224
00:12:56,772 --> 00:12:59,191
These aren't grand gestures.

225
00:12:59,191 --> 00:13:02,277
It's the boring, unsexy stuff.

226
00:13:02,277 --> 00:13:07,324
The stuff that doesn't get a photo op or make it to the grid.

227
00:13:07,324 --> 00:13:11,870
But it's what actually matters in a partnership.

228
00:13:11,870 --> 00:13:16,959
To think I almost lost seven years

229
00:13:16,959 --> 00:13:20,170
of the healthiest love I know

230
00:13:20,170 --> 00:13:27,135
because of a story I made in a snap judgment.

231
00:13:27,135 --> 00:13:31,765
So the question I want to leave you with is this.

232
00:13:31,765 --> 00:13:35,561
What if the filter you're using

233
00:13:35,561 --> 00:13:37,688
to protect yourself

234
00:13:37,688 --> 00:13:41,024
is the very thing that is stopping you

235
00:13:41,024 --> 00:13:44,736
from creating the connection you desire?

236
00:13:44,736 --> 00:13:48,740
Because the right person for you,

237
00:13:48,740 --> 00:13:53,161
they might not come in the package that you expect.

238
00:13:53,161 --> 00:13:56,999
They might be shorter than you planned.

239
00:13:56,999 --> 00:14:00,085
Quieter than you imagined.

240
00:14:00,085 --> 00:14:04,339
Different from your type.

241
00:14:04,339 --> 00:14:11,054
But they might also be kind when you're struggling.

242
00:14:11,054 --> 00:14:15,851
Steady when life gets hard.

243
00:14:15,851 --> 00:14:19,479
The one who shows up again and again,

244
00:14:19,479 --> 00:14:26,320
long after the spark has settled into something deeper.

245
00:14:26,320 --> 00:14:28,196
Don't let a bad photo,

246
00:14:28,196 --> 00:14:29,698
a snap judgment,

247
00:14:29,698 --> 00:14:34,995
a superficial preference cost you that.

248
00:14:34,995 --> 00:14:40,959
Make sure your filter is catching the right things.

249
00:14:40,959 --> 00:14:42,127
Thank you.

250
00:14:42,127 --> 00:14:46,127
(Applause)
